    "divinity": {
      "choice-unfair": "Eating and enjoying comfort can be good. Taking the meal a neighbour needs is wrong because it puts personal comfort above what the neighbour is owed. The fault is in the choice, not in food itself.",
      "not-less-divine": "Augustine argues that the names Father and Son describe a relationship, not greater and lesser kinds of God. A different relationship does not by itself establish an unequal nature. This clarifies his claim without proving it.",
      "gift-common-ground": "Luther and the Council of Trent both deny, in this household comparison, that feeding someone buys the initial gift of being made right with God. This limited agreement does not make their accounts of faith, inward renewal or later good deeds the same.",
      "received-not-benefited": "Luther says an unbelieving Communion guest still receives Christ's body with the bread, because Christ's words establish the gift. Without trusting the promise, the guest does not receive its promised forgiveness. What is given and its benefit to the receiver are separate questions.",
      "bounded-agreement": "At Marburg, the signers affirm one God as Father, Son and Spirit and receiving Christ through faith. They still disagree about his bodily presence in bread and wine. A shared signature records real agreement without erasing its limit.",
      "evidence-not-consensus": "A new record may help establish whose bone the fictional fragment is. Calvin would still reject giving it religious honour even if its identity were proved, while Trent allows honour to genuine relics. Better identity evidence does not settle that religious disagreement.",
      "limited-answer": "Aquinas argues that fire can work well as fire by heating and burning while harming something else. This challenges the claim that every harmful effect needs a wholly evil source. It still leaves the question of why a knowing creator would make such a world.",
      "help-not-erasure": "Augustine says a person still chooses when helped by God. Aquinas says an axe really cuts while a craftsperson works through it. The limited comparison shows that dependent action can be real; it does not reduce people to tools.",
      "reason-boundary": "Aquinas thinks natural reasoning can establish some things about the cause of the world, but not the Trinity. He can therefore explain the belief in one God as Father, Son and Spirit without claiming that ordinary observations prove it.",
      "origins-dispute": "Augustine says evil is damage within a good creation. The writer of the Book of Two Principles argues that a wholly good source cannot cause evil, so evil must have an opposed source. The two accounts give different answers to the same problem of suffering and wrongdoing.",
      "no-paid-shortcut": "Luther describes repentance as a lifetime of turning away from wrong. A seller's promise that a coin guarantees release for a dead soul cannot replace that changed life or give the seller control over God's mercy. His criticism does not declare every church rite or payment fraudulent."
    },
